<section id="fit"><h2>What fits, and what’s tight</h2><div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3 class="ok">Fits well</h3><ul>
<li><b>Steinway B</b> (6′11″ × 4′10″): keys toward the kitchen, tail toward the front windows, lid opening west toward the fireplace lounge. 3′5″ to the lounge, 4′1″ to the stair rail, 5′7″ from the tail to the windows.</li>
<li><b>Cigar chairs + tufted bench</b> face each other across the hearth, about 2½ ft off the fireplace.</li>
<li><b>Glass desk + Aeron</b> in the 9′2″ × 13′6″ secret office, with ~3 ft behind the chair and ~3 ft to the bookcase door.</li>
<li><b>Kids’ room</b> (13′9″ × 13′6″): full bed on the north wall, nightstand, kids desk on the south wall.</li>
<li><b>TV, console, soundbar, sub</b> on the existing family-room TV wall (outlet and cable are already there).</li>
<li><b>Two BendPak HD-9XW lifts</b> (high-rise, standard width, 9,000 lb): left bay, Mustang GT on top of the XT5, mid-lock at about 74 in of drive-under (7.9 in over the 66.1 in XT5). The lift is shifted about 2 ft toward the ridge so the Mustang roof (about 11.25 ft) has about 8.5 in at the left roof edge. Posts are 100.25 in (8.35 ft) and clear the 10 ft walls by about 20 in. Right bay, 911 on top of the Model Y, mid-lock at about 72 in (8.1 in over the 63.9 in Y, no roof rack); also shifted about 2 ft toward the ridge, so the 911 roof (about 10.76 ft) has about 13 in at the right edge.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3 class="tight">Tight / check</h3><ul>
<li><b>Sectional size is unmeasured.</b> The corner spot fits a ~138″ × 86″ L with a 65″ chaise; the short leg must stop ~1 ft before the hinged bookcase so the secret door still works.</li>
<li><b>TV distance:</b> ~15 ft from the back seats (~12 ft from the chaise) to a 75″ screen. If that feels far, float the sectional ~3 ft off the back wall with a sofa table, or go 85″.</li>
<li><b>Massage chair</b> needs ~6½ ft when reclined; it sits on the angle by the bath wall.</li>
<li><b>Piano and sun:</b> keep the tail 5+ ft from the front windows (as shown), use UV film or shades, and add a humidity system (Dampp-Chaser).</li>
<li><b>Do not raise either HD-9XW to the top lock</b> until the ceiling is taped and both lifts are shifted toward the ridge. A left lift centered in the 10×8, even at a mid lock, puts the Mustang into the slope. A right lift left centered and run to the top lock puts the 911 into the slope. Add 2–3 in if the XT5 has roof rails. Confirm the exact top-lock drive-under with BendPak (about 80 in, estimated from the 87 in max lifting height).</li>
<li><b>Door tracks:</b> the left lift sits behind the 10×8 tracks by default (the stack is not in the door-end stall). A jackshaft or wall-mount opener with high-lift track, about $1,500–$3,500 installed per door, is optional if you want that stack in the door-end stall. The right lift stays in the Tesla’s current door-end stall so the Model Y remains a daily in-and-out. Confirm with the installer that the front posts and a car on top clear the 8-ft door’s overhead tracks. If that lift must sit behind the tracks, the Model Y moves in about 10 ft and the gym’s right edge has to shrink.</li>
<li><b>Rope climb</b> at a 13′8″ center is short (about 12–13 ft of rope). Rings and the rope need an engineered strongback, a letter from the truss maker, and a permit — do not hang them from a truss web.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3 class="no">Not placed</h3><ul><li>No dining table, primary bed or guest bed on the moving list, so those rooms show empty in the after photos unless a simple stand-in was used.</li><li>Gym equipment, bikes and the cold plunge go to the garage (see the gym plan).</li></ul></div></div></section>

<section id="move"><h2>Move-in routes for the piano and sectional</h2><div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Piano</h3>Easiest through the <b>6 ft rear French doors</b> in the dining room off the covered deck, then about 15 ft to its spot. Second choice is the 36″ front door with the sidelight; a piano mover takes it in on its side on a board either way. No stairs inside.</div>
<div class="card"><h3>Sectional, route A: main stair</h3>Straight run, 15 risers, ~4′1″ framed width (~3′5″–3′8″ clear), landing in an 8′3″ hall with room to turn, then open into the family room. <b>Best route</b>, but a <b>stair lift</b> shows on this stair in the listing photos; if it stays, its rail costs about a foot of width. Also check the headroom where the stair passes under the main floor.</div>
<div class="card"><h3>Sectional, route B: garage stair</h3>From the right side of the garage: a straight 14-riser stair in a 3′11″ hall, then a <b>90° turn through a ~36″ door</b> at the bottom into the storage room, then a 36″ door into the family room. No finished floors to protect, but the bottom turn is the pinch point: pieces deeper than ~34″ or longer than the landing ceiling may not pivot. Measure before moving day.</div></div></section>

<section id="garage"><h2>Garage: CrossFit gym, two lifts, cold plunge</h2>
<p>The 40×50 heated garage holds the Airstream in the 12×12 center door, daily and weekend cars on two 4-post lifts, and an L-shaped gym of about <b>660 sf</b> across the back and left side. Permit drawings and the truss layout show <b>10 ft walls</b> and 12″ heeled modified scissor (cathedral) trusses, with a flat center of about <b>13′8″</b> (likely range 13′4″–14′8″ — tape it; the exterior ridge is about 17½–18 ft, which is why it feels taller). The rig, rings and rope sit under that center. There are three wall-ball targets, a clear handstand wall, a cardio row with a TV, and the Rogue Donkey. Two 8-ft Monster 3-tier dumbbell racks and a 76.5″ kettlebell rack sit along the left gym wall, with two plate trees and three bar hangers by the platform, so a full 5–100 lb dumbbell set, competition kettlebell pairs, and three bars loaded to 315 can live in the 660 sf gym. The cold plunge sits beside the gym. A 6.5×5.5 office is on the gym side of the house door. The 3 ft walkway to the basement stair stays open.</p>
<p>All five vehicles park <b>nose-in</b>: noses toward the house, rears toward the doors. The left 10×8 gets a BendPak HD-9XW behind the door tracks, shifted about 2 ft toward the ridge, with the <b>black Cadillac XT5</b> under and the <b>black Mustang GT</b> on top, locked at about 74 in of drive-under. The right 10×8 gets a second HD-9XW in the Tesla’s current stall, also shifted about 2 ft toward the ridge, with the <b>white Tesla Model Y</b> under and the <b>matte black 911 Carrera 4 GTS</b> on top, locked at about 72 in. The <b>silver 2022 Airstream Interstate 24X</b> (Mercedes Sprinter 3500 4x4) sits in the 12×12, nudged slightly right so it clears the shifted left lift. Do not use the top lock on either lift until the ceiling is taped. The Tesla Wall Connector moves to the <b>garage-door end of the house wall</b>, so the cable reaches around the rear to the Model Y’s driver-side port when the car is nose-in under the lift. (The earlier mid-wall placement was a long walk for that port.) The HD-9XW is 208–230V; plan two 240V circuits and a garage subpanel.</p>
