<section id="sauna"><h2>9×16 wood-fired sauna, perpendicular to the deck</h2>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-backyard.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-backyard.jpg" alt="Concept: 9x16 wood-fired skid sauna extending straight out from the deck at the hot-tub end"></a><figcaption>Concept: the 9×16 wood-fired skid sauna extends straight out from the deck at the hot-tub end, so it does not sit in front of the rear windows. The changing room and the 32-inch door are at the deck; the stove and a short Class-A chimney are at the far / yard end. Sizes in this edited photo are approximate — the site plan and elevation below are the source of truth.</figcaption></figure><figure class="listing-only"><a href="photos/p59.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="photos/p59.jpg" alt="Same view today (listing photo)"></a><figcaption>Same view today (listing photo)</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="fig2" style="margin-top:14px"><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-deck.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-deck.jpg" alt="Closer view: 9x16 skid sauna butted to the deck, chimney at the far yard end"></a><figcaption>Closer view of the same siting: the 9-ft end butts to the front of the deck, the 16 ft runs into the yard, and the short Class-A chimney is at the far / yard end. The long bedroom window stays clear. Sizes are approximate; use the drawings for exact dimensions.</figcaption></figure><figure class="listing-only"><a href="photos/p02.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="photos/p02.jpg" alt="Same closer view today (listing photo)"></a><figcaption>Same closer view today (listing photo)</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="fig2" style="margin-top:14px"><figure><a href="img/site-plan-sauna.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/site-plan-sauna.jpg" alt="Dimensioned site plan: 9x16 wood-fired skid sauna perpendicular to the deck"></a><figcaption>Dimensioned site plan (concept, to scale): the exact 9′-0″ × 16′-0″ sauna, 16 ft perpendicular to the deck at the hot-tub end. Changing room at the deck; hot room and chimney at the far / yard end. Drain field and tank are on the west / garage side. The sketched reserve may overlap the far end — have a pumper mark it before staking.</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="img/sauna-deck-elevation.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/sauna-deck-elevation.jpg" alt="Elevation: covered deck and 9x16 sauna extending into the yard, chimney at the far end"></a><figcaption>Elevation from the east (concept, to scale): the 16 ft runs straight out from the 31′-0″ covered deck, floors matched at about 1′-6″ above grade (tape the deck). The flue is at the far / yard end, about 28 ft from the house, so the 3-ft-above-roof rule governs — cap about 11 ft above grade, pending a field tape.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
<div class="card"><h3>Siting</h3><ul>
<li>The <b>9-ft entry wall is butted</b> to the south / yard edge of the covered deck, at the southeast corner next to the hot tub, with <b>zero gap</b>. You step from the deck straight into the 32″ outswing door. Align the door with the ~3.5 ft walkway at the front of the tub. Relocate the cedar privacy screen now at the east edge so it does not block the door or the walkway.</li>
<li>The <b>16 ft runs perpendicular to the deck, straight out into the yard</b>. About 5′-4″ of the 9-ft entry wall is flush to the south fascia; the remaining 3′-8″ continues just past the east corner. That offset keeps the building out of every rear window: kitchen, French doors, walk-in closet, and the primary bedroom. An earlier along-the-house placement sat in front of the bedroom window and is not used.</li>
<li>From the deck out, the interior follows the original set: a <b>changing room 4′-6″ × 8′-1″</b> at the deck end, then a <b>hot room 10′-1 ½″ × 8′-1″</b> toward the yard. Two double-hung windows are in the changing room (one on each side). Two tempered picture windows are in the hot room (far wall and low long wall). The stove and chimney are at the far / yard end.</li>
<li>The permit first-floor plan (sheet A4) shows the deck as <b>31′-0″ wide by about 11 ft deep</b>, on 6×6 posts with 5¼×11⅞ PSL beams and 5/4 wood decking. The included hot tub already sits in the east bay. The sauna has its own gravel-pad foundation — <b>no load on the deck</b>. Flash the joint, including the 6″ roof overhang that will sit over the deck.</li>
<li>Foundation is three 6×6 pressure-treated skids on a compacted gravel pad. This lawn is relatively flat, so piers are not expected unless a tape shows a slope.</li>
<li>Whitewater Township RC-1 setbacks are 15 ft side and 30 ft rear, with a 35 ft height limit. Woodland Trails allows accessory buildings 75 ft back from the road right-of-way. The house is about 85 ft from the south line, so about 57 ft remains beyond the sauna — tape the south and east lot lines before staking.</li>
<li>The drain field and tank are on the <b>west / garage side</b> of the house, clear of this corner. A sketched reserve in the backyard is roughly 15–30 ft off the house (about y = −31.5 to −16.7 in the model, ±10–15 ft) and may overlap the far ~11 ft of this sauna. <b>Have a pumper mark the tank, field and reserve first, then stake.</b> Keep the sauna and its pad off the marked field and tank.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>The pick: the original 9×16 wood-fired skid sauna</h3><ul>
<li>The exact <b>9′-0″ × 16′-0″</b> outdoor skid sauna framing set (not the earlier 8×12 scale-down). Roof with overhangs 17′-0″ × 10′-6″; height to the top of the 2.5:12 shingle shed is 9′-9 7/16″. Board-and-batten siding, 2×6 walls at 16″ with mineral-wool (R-23). Changing room 4′-6″ × 8′-1″ at the deck/door end, with a double-hung window on each side; hot room 10′-1 ½″ × 8′-1″ (~540 cu ft) toward the yard, with two tempered picture windows (far wall and low long wall) and seating for about 8–10.</li>
<li>Wood-fired stove, <b>NIPPA WB-21 or equivalent</b>, purchased with the 12″ thru-wall firing extension so it is fired from outside through the far / yard wall, away from the deck and the house. Use the maker’s listed clearances, an FRT 2×6 zone, protected-wall heat shields, cement board, and fiber-cement at the firing door. The stove is about $2,299, or $2,450 with the firing extension, plus a 6″ Class-A chimney kit, a non-combustible hearth pad, heat shields and a spark arrestor — stove and chimney about $3,000–$4,500.</li>
<li>Height budget above the bottom of the skids: 5½″ skid + 5½″ joist + ¾″ subfloor + plates and 91⅝″ studs + rafter/roof ≈ 9′-9 7/16″ at the high side. Finished floor is about 13″ above the skid bottom. <b>Do not deepen the skids or joists</b> without re-checking that roof height. Raise the gravel pad instead so the finished floor matches the deck (about 1′-6″ above grade — tape the deck; the skid bottom then sits about 5″ above grade).</li>
<li>DIY from the original 9×16 framing set: about <b>$12,000–$22,000</b> in materials, plus the stove and chimney. Builder-built: about <b>$28,000–$50,000</b>.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Chimney, hearth, clearances, power, and permits</h3><ul>
<li><b>6″ Class-A insulated chimney</b> at the far / yard end, with a support box, roof boot, removable top sections, and a <b>spark arrestor</b> on the cap. Code rule used here: the top is at least <b>3 ft above the sauna roof and 2 ft above anything within 10 ft</b>. The flue is about 28 ft from the house wall (12 ft of deck plus 16 ft of sauna), so the house 6/12 roof is <b>not</b> within 10 ft and the 3-ft-above-sauna-roof rule governs. Plan the cap at about <b>11 ft above grade</b> (about 3 ft of pipe above the low eave), pending a field tape of the low roof and anything else within 10 ft (trees). This replaces an earlier ~15 ft cap that applied when the flue sat only a few feet from the house.</li>
<li><b>Non-combustible hearth pad</b> under the stove and at the outside firing door, sized to the stove maker’s instructions, plus the listed heat shields and wall/ceiling clearances. Don’t sit the stove on the wood skids alone.</li>
<li>Keep a clear working strip around the firing door, and don’t store firewood against the sauna wall that has the stove.</li>
<li>No 240-volt circuit is required. A short 120V/20A run for a light and an outlet is optional; it can come from the nearby deck or house circuits.</li>
<li>An accessory-building permit, and a wood-stove / chimney permit, should be confirmed with Whitewater Township and the county. Tell the insurer. Concept only; not engineered or stamped.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Buildability versus the original 9×16 set</h3>
<p>The exact 9×16 framing set <b>is buildable</b> at this corner if the 9-ft end is butted to the deck and the 16 ft runs perpendicular, out into the yard. Specs that fit as drawn, and the few adjustments this site needs:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fits:</b> 9′-0″ × 16′-0″ framing, 17′-0″ × 10′-6″ roof, 9′-9 7/16″ height, 2.5:12 shed, three 6×6 PT skids, 32″ outswing door on the deck end, changing room and hot room sizes and windows as drawn, NIPPA WB-21 (or equivalent) with listed clearances, west-side drain field and tank (clear), RC-1 rear setback at this corner (about 57 ft remains to the south line — tape it), HOA road setback, and a separate gravel-pad foundation on this relatively flat lawn. No rear house window is blocked.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — orientation:</b> run the 16 ft straight out from the southeast corner of the deck, not along the house. Shift the 9-ft entry wall slightly past the east corner (about 5′-4″ on the fascia, 3′-8″ past it) so the building stays out of the walk-in-closet and bedroom windows.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — door and floor:</b> door faces the deck (not the yard); raise the pad so the finished floor matches the deck (about 1′-6″ a.g., tape it). Do not deepen the skids or joists.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — chimney:</b> because the flue is at the far / yard end, about 28 ft from the house, the 3-ft-above-sauna-roof rule governs. Plan the cap at about 11 ft a.g., pending a tape of the low roof and of anything else within 10 ft.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — joint:</b> relocate the east-edge privacy screen; flash the 6″ roof overhang that will sit over the deck; do not ledger or bear any sauna load on the deck framing.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — reserve:</b> the sketched reserve may overlap the far ~11 ft of the sauna (±10–15 ft). Have a pumper mark the field, tank and reserve first, then stake. Keep the foundation off the marked field and tank.</li>
</ul></div></div></section>

